  "query_preview": "..(Monday through Saturday)\nIssued at 157 PM EST SAT NOV 14 2026\n\nAt the start of the long term period, 500 mb troughing will be \ncentered over the Atlantic Coast, with expansive ridging over much\nof the interior United States, excluding a cutoff low initially \nlocated in the ArkLaTex vicinity. Farther west, troughing will be \nlocated over the Pacific Coast, and as this trough digs towards \nthe Southwest, it will force the Central US ridge eastward. \nMeanwhile, the cutoff low will weaken and drift southeast before \nit is eventually absorbed into the broader Atlantic trough. Even \nas these features progress, ridging will persist locally, with \njust a lone shortwave trough translating northeast along the ridge\nperiphery on Thursday and Friday, bringing no impacts to the \nforecast area. At the surface, this pattern will maintain high \npressure near the forecast area for the duration of the long term \nperiod, with no other notable surface features approaching eastern\nKentucky. Accordingly, weather in the long term period looks to \nbe uneventful, with no precipitation expected. \n\nAs midlevel heights rise through midweek, temperatures will trend\nwarmer. Highs in the 50s on Monday will climb to the mid 60s by \nWednesday, nearly 10 degrees above average. At this point, LREF \nmean 500 mb heights look to be firmly above the 95th percentile \ncompared to climatology, indicating the anomalous strength of this\nridge. As the 500 mb ridge axis shifts east of the forecast area,\nlittle \"relief\" from above average temperatures is expected. The \neastward shift of the associated surface high will lead to \nsouthwesterly winds locally, advecting warm and more moist air \ninto the forecast area. Accordingly, temperatures could climb into\nthe low 70s by the end of the period. Dewpoints should rebound \nsomewhat, too; initially, afternoon dewpoints will be quite dry, \nin the upper 20s and low 30s, with the lowest minimum RH values \nexpected to be in the 25-30% range on Wednesday and Thursday \n(although light winds should work to mitigate fire weather \nconcerns). However, as winds become more southwesterly, dewpoints \nwill rise, reaching into the 40s by the end of the period. This \nincreased moisture should allow a few clouds to return to the \nforecast by Thursday (marking a transition from \"sunny\" to \"mostly\nsunny\"), but otherwise, impacts will be minimal. Expect low \ntemperatures to follow this warming trend as well, although robust\nridge/valley temperature splits will develop each night as clear \nskies and a weak pressure gradient remain in place. As cold air \ndrains into the valleys, these locations could see temperatures in\nthe low to mid 20s on Tuesday morning. By the end of the period, \nhowever, valley lows will climb into the upper 30s and low 40s, \nwhile overnight temperatures will consistently remain warmer on \nridges.",
